1
00:03:42,857 --> 00:03:48,857
It's the story of a man, a woman, and a rabbit in a triangle of trouble.

2
00:07:25,714 --> 00:07:31,714
Who Framed Roger Rabbit is a fantasy, animation,
comedy film directed by Robert Zemeckis.

3
00:11:08,571 --> 00:11:14,571
'Toon star Roger is worried that his wife Jessica is playing pattycake with someone
else, so the studio hires detective Eddie Valiant to snoop on her.

4
00:14:51,428 --> 00:14:57,428
But the stakes are quickly raised when Marvin Acme is
found dead and Roger is the prime suspect.

5
00:18:34,285 --> 00:18:40,285
Eddie Valiant, played by Bob Hoskins.

6
00:22:17,142 --> 00:22:23,142
Judge Doom, played by Christopher Lloyd.

7
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:06,000
Dolores, played by Joanna Cassidy.

8
00:29:42,857 --> 00:29:48,857
Roger Rabbit / Benny The Cab / Greasy / Psycho
(voice), played by Charles Fleischer.

9
00:33:25,714 --> 00:33:31,714
Jessica Rabbit (voice) (uncredited), played by Kathleen Turner.

10
00:37:08,571 --> 00:37:14,571
Marvin Acme, played by Stubby Kaye.

11
00:40:51,428 --> 00:40:57,428
Who Framed Roger Rabbit is a 1988 American fantasy comedy film directed by Robert
Zemeckis from a screenplay written by Jeffrey Price and Peter S.

12
00:44:34,285 --> 00:44:40,285
Combining live-action and animation, it is based on the
1981 novel Who Censored Roger Rabbit?

13
00:48:17,142 --> 00:48:23,142
The film stars Bob Hoskins, Christopher Lloyd, Stubby Kaye, and Joanna
Cassidy, with Charles Fleischer providing the voice of Roger Rabbit.

14
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
Set in an alternative history Hollywood in 1947, where humans and cartoon characters (referred to as "toons") co-exist, Who Framed Roger Rabbit
follows Eddie Valiant, a private investigator with a grudge against toons, who must help exonerate Roger, who has been framed for murder.

15
00:55:42,857 --> 00:55:48,857
Walt Disney Studios purchased the film rights for the story in 1981.

16
00:59:25,714 --> 00:59:31,714
Price and Seaman wrote two drafts of the script before Disney brought in executive
producer Steven Spielberg and his production company, Amblin Entertainment.

17
01:03:08,571 --> 01:03:14,571
Zemeckis was brought on to direct, and Canadian-British animator Richard
Williams was hired to supervise the animation sequences.

18
01:06:51,428 --> 01:06:57,428
Production was moved from Los Angeles to Elstree Studios in England
to accommodate Williams and his group of animators.

19
01:10:34,285 --> 01:10:40,285
While filming, the production budget rapidly expanded, and the
shooting schedule ran longer than expected.

20
01:14:17,142 --> 01:14:23,142
Who Framed Roger Rabbit was released through Disney's Touchstone
Pictures banner in the United States on June 22, 1988.

21
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
The film received critical acclaim for its visuals, humor, writing,
performances, and groundbreaking combination of live-action and animation.

22
01:21:42,857 --> 01:21:48,857
It grossed over $351 million worldwide, becoming the
second-highest-grossing film of 1988, behind Rain Man.

23
01:25:25,714 --> 01:25:31,714
It brought a renewed interest in the golden age of American animation,
spearheading modern American animation and the Disney Renaissance.

24
01:29:08,571 --> 01:29:14,571
It won three Academy Awards for Best Film Editing, Best Sound Effects Editing and Best Visual Effects
and received a Special Achievement Academy Award for Williams' animation direction.

25
01:32:51,428 --> 01:32:57,428
In 2016, the film was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

26
01:36:34,285 --> 01:36:40,285
The film explores themes of lovesickness, based on novel or
book, falsely accused, movie business, innocence.

27
01:40:17,142 --> 01:40:23,142
The tone is lighthearted and dark.
